1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:31,570
Welcome to Update 20 of AHEAD Pathway. The Rehearsal room has learned from your first rounds in it. Two things stood out: spoken answers often ran long, and took a while to reach the point. That is natural under pressure, and it is exactly what practice can change. So we have built three improvements: coaching on how you deliver your answers, a direct link from the room to your evidence map, and a way to tell us honestly what helps.

2
00:00:32,969 --> 00:01:05,609
When you answer aloud, the ring around the microphone now fills at two minutes, the length a real panel usually expects. The reminder is simple: say your answer in your first sentence, then give the reason, and one example from your study. There is no hard stop. And notice the label above this question: Your evidence map. The panel now knows which parts of your study still have little evidence, and in every round it asks about one of them, just as a real panel would.

3
00:01:07,009 --> 00:01:29,839
Your debrief now begins with how you delivered your answers: the average length, how many of your answers led with the answer itself, whether you repeated yourself, and how this round compares with your last one. Here, the average came down from four minutes to one and a half. That is what improvement looks like, and now you can see it.

4
00:01:31,239 --> 00:01:49,009
Each question carries a short note: how long you spoke, whether you led with the answer, and whether the answer went round in circles. Then choose Practise this question again, and the same panel member asks it once more, and follows up, until it is clear.

5
00:01:50,409 --> 00:02:11,939
This is the part we are most excited about. When an answer was hard, the debrief now shows which part of your study it belongs to, and how many readings your map holds there. Very often, the question you struggled to answer is exactly where your evidence is thinnest. One click opens that part of your map.

6
00:02:13,340 --> 00:02:39,409
Your map opens with that part highlighted, and a note on what to do next. Add a reading you have found yourself, or write a note on the readings already there. And next Monday, the readings chosen for you lean towards that part. Reading, thinking and speaking now work as one loop: what you found hard to say becomes what you read next, and what you read makes the next answer stronger.

7
00:02:40,810 --> 00:03:05,539
Finally, after each debrief you are asked one question: how useful was this round, from one to five, with a line if you would like to say what would make it better. Your answer is kept without your name. The Director sees only the totals and the comments, never who wrote them. So please be candid: it is how the room keeps getting better for you.

8
00:03:06,939 --> 00:03:28,609
Answer first. See where your evidence is thin. Read your way to a stronger answer, and tell us honestly what helps. Fifteen minutes in the Rehearsal room, once a week, and you will hear the difference. That is AHEAD Pathway: your degree, well supported, every step of the way.
